---
import AegisGlass from "../../../components/studies/gilded/AegisGlass.svelte";
import LhiGlass from "../../../components/studies/gilded/LhiGlass.svelte";
import ScoutGlass from "../../../components/studies/gilded/ScoutGlass.svelte";
import SurfaceAtlas from "../../../components/studies/gilded/SurfaceAtlas.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "gilded-surfaces-2026-11-03",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / A material language across shapes</h2>
    <p>
      The Fiddler reference gives cockpit glass a distinctive material: gold at
      the forward tip, a space-dark middle and green toward the rear. Its
      central ridge divides the reflection into two surfaces. The desired result
      of this study is a clear visual-language guide that explains how to use
      that green–space–gold treatment on round, curved, cornered and faceted
      surfaces, with every ship’s cockpit as a practical application.
    </p>
    <p>
      The central tension is continuity versus form. A recognizable material
      should survive a change of shape, but a rounded canopy should not look
      like a diamond with softened corners. We need to distinguish what stays
      constant —the colour family, dark depth and balance of reflection—from
      what follows the object: highlight position, transitions, seams and
      changes of plane. The reference suggests a direction; these rules remain
      to be discovered.
    </p>
    <p>
      The guide should pair rules with rendered examples: a round dome, a long
      curved canopy, an angular canopy and a multi-plane surface, followed by
      the actual cockpit family at enlarged and flight sizes. It should explain
      where the material belongs, how adjacent surfaces meet, and when restraint
      preserves the ship’s character. Extending it onto hull panels is an open
      usage question. The current art direction calls for flat fills and black
      canopies; any proposed exception must be explicit in the eventual scope.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>02 / Reflection follows the surface</h2>
    <details>
      <summary>
        02.1 / One material, four constructions — approved direction
      </summary>
      <p>
        First proposal: gold catches the forward light, green returns at the
        rear, and a broad space-dark band gives the glass depth. Curvature bends
        the band; a real ridge breaks it. An angular outline alone does neither.
      </p>
      <SurfaceAtlas />
      <p>
        Designer decision: the four surface treatments in 02.1 are approved as
        the material direction. Cockpit application and small-scale readability
        remain open.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are procedural material sketches under a fixed forward-left light,
        not ship geometry or a physical reflection simulation. The round and
        curved samples test continuous roll-off; the final sample interprets the
        Fiddler reference’s split planes. Colour, band width and contrast are
        provisional.
      </p>
      <p>
        Proposed usage boundary: reserve this rich reflection for glass first.
        Keep surrounding structure quiet so the dark centre still reads as
        depth. Applying it to opaque panels remains a separate comparison.
        Cockpit fit and flight-size readability have not yet been demonstrated.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details>
      <summary>02.2 / Daehan: space glass dome — approved</summary>
      <p>
        Designer decision: gradient direction −70°, reflection edge direction
        −30°, gold border retained. This reads as a space glass dome and is
        approved as the visual language for the Daehan family. Scout is the
        reviewed example; fitting this language to the other Daehan cockpit
        geometries remains work.
      </p>
      <ScoutGlass client:load />
      <p>
        The accepted construction combines a green–space–gold gradient, dark
        edge roll-off, a gold collar and a crisp bowed reflection crossing glass
        and collar. Gradient and reflection rotate independently. Reset restores
        the accepted values; adjustments here are temporary explorations.
      </p>
      <p>
        The designer has directed exploration to continue with LHI next.
        Daehan’s implementation readiness, production integration and flight
        review remain to be assessed. Visual approval does not claim a deployed
        change.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <details>
      <summary>02.3 / LHI: cut glass at the ridge — B selected</summary>
      <p>
        Wasp’s two production canopy planes provide the first LHI comparison.
        The material keeps the green–space–gold family but replaces the dome’s
        bowed reflection with straight edges on each plane. A thin gold
        perimeter frames the glass; the centre ridge remains a meeting of
        surfaces.
      </p>
      <LhiGlass client:load />
      <p>
        A keeps the reflection connected at the ridge and changes its direction.
        B adds a step between planes, testing a stronger difference in reflected
        view. The question is whether that step gives the canopy more solidity
        or makes it feel like two unrelated panes. Designer decision: B is
        selected; retain the reflection offset between panes.
      </p>
      <p>
        These are study overlays on the real Wasp hull and fittings. The
        proposed gold perimeter and reflection are not production changes.
        Fiddler application follows after the pane relationship is settled;
        static views cannot establish readability in motion.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>02.4 / The selected reflection on both LHI ships</h3>
    <p>
      B’s offset reflection now carries across Wasp and Fiddler. Both use their
      production glass outlines, the same palette, right-pane shading and thin
      gold perimeter. The offset remains 13% of canopy length. This checks the
      selected pane relationship against Fiddler’s larger surrounding hull.
    </p>
    <LhiGlass family client:load />
    <p>
      Designer decision: the B treatment on Wasp and Fiddler is approved for
      LHI, including the offset reflection, darker right pane and thin gold
      perimeter. Production integration and flight verification remain separate
      work.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>02.5 / Aegis: blue visor — approved</h3>
    <p>
      Designer decision: continuous visor, gradient −79°, reflection −39°, blue
      reflection (#427fe0) at 32% opacity, thin gold perimeter retained. The
      remaining ship family is excluded from this cycle.
    </p>
    <AegisGlass client:load />
    <p>
      Reset restores the accepted values. The study and production now consume
      the same material renderer. The reflection stays crisp and rotates
      separately from the green–space–gold gradient.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>03 / Ready for shared rendering</h2>
    <p>
      Scout, XF-1, Wasp and Fiddler already share drawBuild across the Hangar,
      Lab and flight. Their existing cockpit geometry supplies the clipping
      paths; no silhouette, fitting or simulation change is required. The
      accepted procedural Canvas treatments can be extracted directly and
      painted after hull geometry, before fittings. Readiness: no implementation
      blocker.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shared material stays in ship coordinates, as reviewed; it rotates
      with the ship rather than tracking a world light. Gradients are authored
      reflections, not physical environment maps. The existing flight scale is
      preserved. Automated checks cannot establish feel, readability amid
      effects or sustained device performance.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>04 / Deliver the three approved families</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Art-direction change:</strong>
      permit gradients and crisp reflective overlays for approved cockpit
      materials, plus restrained gold framing. Hull planes retain flat fills.
      This replaces the universal black-canopy rule for Daehan, LHI and Aegis
      only, under the designer’s implementation authorization.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Small: extract the accepted glass renderers and parameters into mode-a;
        keep the Lab study controls on the same definitions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Small: integrate them in drawBuild before fittings. Cover Scout, XF-1,
        Wasp and Fiddler; leave Mark II Escort Frigate unchanged.
      </li>
      <li>
        Small: document manufacturer usage rules, check types, build and
        rendering integration; assess human flight fidelity separately.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Local implementation is connected. This study is accepted; it does not
      claim a push, deployment or completed human flight review. Validation
      results are reported with the delivery.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>
